<!--
    Wide-slice SSA reduction, `option wideslice on|off`. The witness is clang -O3
    x86-64: eight input bytes are widened through SSE unpack operations,
    accumulated in four dword lanes, folded horizontally, then followed by a
    scalar tail.

    Heritage widens each one- or two-byte lane write by joining it with the
    bytes below it, so the XMM value passes through intermediates 2..15 bytes
    wide, and the rule pool keeps recreating such widths while it folds the zero
    lanes. Nothing upstream takes a 13-byte value apart again.

    Pass 1 (`option wideslice off`): assertions #1 and #2 reproduce the bug,
    six 13-byte locals that exist nowhere in the program and ten lines of
    SUB13x reads of them. Pass 2 (default on): assertions #3 to #5 prove every
    lane sum is a direct shift-and-mask of the loaded dword; none of those
    shapes occurs in pass 1.

    This self-contained byte_sum regression embeds 143 bytes of x86-64 code
    loaded at 0x1000.
-->
